hoices ["answer-60efeb69-2291-5ab8-97b8-0921d4ea912c" "answer-ab7954bb-6098-5d17-87fa-f7398cad0ac1" "answer-2a8ec316-eac3-5582-ac9e-9d0ecabb5dd5" "answer-5454a88a-d451-5112-aaba-edf60456ae48" "answer-8a15ef33-1e8c-55f7-81a5-4f5dc6a79003"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60efeb69-2291-5ab8-97b8-0921d4ea912c" :answer/id #uuid "60efeb69-2291-5ab8-97b8-0921d4ea912c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab7954bb-6098-5d17-87fa-f7398cad0ac1" :answer/id #uuid "ab7954bb-6098-5d17-87fa-f7398cad0ac1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a8ec316-eac3-5582-ac9e-9d0ecabb5dd5" :answer/id #uuid "2a8ec316-eac3-5582-ac9e-9d0ecabb5dd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{56}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5454a88a-d451-5112-aaba-edf60456ae48" :answer/id #uuid "5454a88a-d451-5112-aaba-edf60456ae48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a15ef33-1e8c-55f7-81a5-4f5dc6a79003" :answer/id #uuid "8a15ef33-1e8c-55f7-81a5-4f5dc6a79003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{56}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70693bce-1563-5043-b145-f1732cc4b8df" :question/id #uuid "70693bce-1563-5043-b145-f1732cc4b8df" :question/math-academy-id "q-67874" :question/problem "A race car covers $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a lap in  $2$ minutes.  What is the unit rate of laps covered per minute?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of laps per minute, we divide the number of laps, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of seconds, $2$, as follows:     \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷2\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷2 & =\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 1}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{5}$ laps per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-728455ea-fb60-53bd-a006-dfa511296e5c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-728455ea-fb60-53bd-a006-dfa511296e5c" :answer-field/id #uuid "728455ea-fb60-53bd-a006-dfa511296e5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aec67466-3054-5dc0-beb6-55abf9092bc6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a01ebef-f9b5-5146-8ea2-b0be45e8fdd3" "answer-31a2c1e0-465b-5463-ada3-ebc481d00702" "answer-3b52d182-7e17-5949-be54-a233c9d261b3" "answer-aec67466-3054-5dc0-beb6-55abf9092bc6" "answer-c5f5b6f1-8864-5516-86ca-c909870e413c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a01ebef-f9b5-5146-8ea2-b0be45e8fdd3" :answer/id #uuid "9a01ebef-f9b5-5146-8ea2-b0be45e8fdd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\frac{3}{5}$ laps per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31a2c1e0-465b-5463-ada3-ebc481d00702" :answer/id #uuid "31a2c1e0-465b-5463-ada3-ebc481d00702" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{10}$ laps per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b52d182-7e17-5949-be54-a233c9d261b3" :answer/id #uuid "3b52d182-7e17-5949-be54-a233c9d261b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{10}$ laps per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aec67466-3054-5dc0-beb6-55abf9092bc6" :answer/id #uuid "aec67466-3054-5dc0-beb6-55abf9092bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{5}$ laps per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5f5b6f1-8864-5516-86ca-c909870e413c" :answer/id #uuid "c5f5b6f1-8864-5516-86ca-c909870e413c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{5}$ laps per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bde894c0-0f60-52a1-8f53-44362f2c04ac" :question/id #uuid "bde894c0-0f60-52a1-8f53-44362f2c04ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-71431" :question/problem "An air compressor moves $\\frac{8}{15}$ gallons of air in four seconds. Find the unit rate of gallons per second." :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of gallons per second, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{8}{15},$ by the number of seconds, $4$, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{15}÷4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{15}÷4 & =\\frac{8}{15}÷\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\times 1}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 4}{15\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times \\cancel{4}}{15\\times \\cancel{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{2}{15}$ gallons per second." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3757453c-451a-533e-89bf-ae008505f529"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3757453c-451a-533e-89bf-ae008505f529" :answer-field/id #uuid "3757453c-451a-533e-89bf-ae008505f529" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbc7a1c4-f866-5d17-a6b7-c95942a60d35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ea4677e-c8ef-54b9-a15a-630f36cbe00b" "answer-8cc41ae4-5ed6-5660-bbbc-4a08a3bf722b" "answer-cbc7a1c4-f866-5d17-a6b7-c95942a60d35" "answer-9b42d88b-d93e-501b-b9d6-31e30b995f77" "answer-8d75af3a-b98d-5d57-aa31-3ac9a9e6b286"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea4677e-c8ef-54b9-a15a-630f36cbe00b" :answer/id #uuid "8ea4677e-c8ef-54b9-a15a-630f36cbe00b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}$ gallons per second" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cc41ae4-5ed6-5660-bbbc-4a08a3bf722b" :answer/id #uuid "8cc41ae4-5ed6-5660-bbbc-4a08a3bf722b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}$ gallons per second" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbc7a1c4-f866-5d17-a6b7-c95942a60d35" :answer/id #uuid "cbc7a1c4-f866-5d17-a6b7-c95942a60d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{15}$ gallons per second" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b42d88b-d93e-501b-b9d6-31e30b995f77" :answer/id #uuid "9b42d88b-d93e-501b-b9d6-31e30b995f77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{15}$ gallons per second" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d75af3a-b98d-5d57-aa31-3ac9a9e6b286" :answer/id #uuid "8d75af3a-b98d-5d57-aa31-3ac9a9e6b286" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{15}$ gallons per second" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152647 :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of gallons per minute, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{9}{10},$ by the number of minutes, $3$, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{10}÷3\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}÷3 & =\\frac{9}{10}÷\\frac{3}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 1}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{3}}{10\\times \\cancel{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{3}{10}$ gallons per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150389 :answer-field/correct 17592186150391 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" :question/id #uuid "06090a54-3bf7-5dce-b7f7-51954f025453" :question/math-academy-id "q-71434" :question/problem "A container with $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of weed killer can spray $\\frac{7}{4}$ lawns. What is the unit rate of gallons per lawn?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of gallons per lawn, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{3}{4},$ by the number of lawns, $\\frac{7}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{7}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 4}{4\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1" :answer-field/id #uuid "09020a00-7821-5c7f-a323-c2393071bde1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" "answer-1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" "answer-ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" "answer-01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" :answer/id #uuid "16a97f78-f4fe-5855-b81b-381b3a37c9e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" :answer/id #uuid "1005ee30-9311-5fb9-a232-e0178b92429a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer/id #uuid "7ebc5524-a707-5058-b0e2-7b06a1664d65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{7}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" :answer/id #uuid "ff687e65-7e6f-5e64-a0f5-de85e7a030e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{7}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f" :answer/id #uuid "01f2bbf6-d10e-547b-9978-414fb88af65f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{3}$ gallons per lawn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/id #uuid "6cccdce8-916f-545e-8523-5e2c1794424a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67967" :question/problem "Riding his electric bike, Matt can go $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a mile in $\\frac{3}{4}$  minutes. What is the unit rate of miles covered per minute?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{3}{4}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2}{2\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2f73d-2fec-566e-a93a-03901ddd1e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" "answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" "answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" "answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/id #uuid "34d51373-e059-5bb2-b915-ea1bf3300d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ mile per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/id #uuid "08d6df6d-3090-5380-af3f-b6a2c1297623" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{3}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/id #uuid "d6bb8ba7-cf6b-5bb3-aa7f-0dc97af0d2b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/id #uuid "4bc6dd10-ce27-56ea-8697-58814869e5a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/id #uuid "a26136a8-2a89-5671-9dc1-9250849e70bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{6}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/id #uuid "37159145-3e5f-53f2-822d-4367df7cb7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-67957" :question/problem "A skydiver falls $\\frac{4}{5}$ of a mile in $\\frac{2}{5}$ minutes. What is the unit rate of miles fallen per minute?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per minute, we divide the number of miles, $\\frac{4}{5},$ by the number of minutes, $\\frac{2}{5}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 5}{5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}\\times 2}{\\cancel{2}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $2$ miles per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/id #uuid "f44b7de9-d96a-53e8-a12e-ce4a8ea8e840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" "answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" "answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" "answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" "answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/id #uuid "a70b8aba-2006-588a-9b36-9664101c1dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/id #uuid "d42f7600-375a-59ca-9455-2853caf93570" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{10}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/id #uuid "70090ca7-e591-51bc-b92a-43641ff66542" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/id #uuid "493940a9-253b-5a45-a88e-7e139a8230c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{10}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/id #uuid "d43afe7e-a4ce-5422-b4f9-1126acce88dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{5}$ miles per minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/id #uuid "6aea863a-357f-52de-8ad7-b9c9c32993ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-67962" :question/problem "A certain satellite makes $\\frac{1}{3}$  of its orbit around the Earth in $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a day. How many times can it orbit Earth in $1$ day?" :question/worked-solution "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ec2a288-b18b-538e-8e74-f5770e5218c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" "answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" "answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" "answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" "answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/id #uuid "41a64a33-32be-5dd3-a470-cc04307cda2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/id #uuid "506250f5-ba3b-5016-bdc4-5b2f75606b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/id #uuid "8d0a7fb2-745a-5a4b-8c76-c522f2f54c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/id #uuid "663127ee-ca61-54fd-bd7a-e8c3c21cf07f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/id #uuid "926c20da-babe-5216-892f-00c1074d45bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152651 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles Jim can run in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{5}{6},$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{5},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5} & =\\frac{5}{6}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{6\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jim can run $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ miles in $1$ hour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150413 :answer-field/correct "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" "answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/id #uuid "d04bb74e-c21f-56f1-9310-a6adde831b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/id #uuid "0153e8af-7940-50ee-90ef-b1dfd0e45b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/id #uuid "148f688d-3a85-57a5-b966-a8820f8f1a74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153341 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100÷15 & =\\frac{100}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{100÷5}{15÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run $6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ meters per second." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152308 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186114133 :answer-field/choices 17592186114138] {:db/id 17592186115383 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115868 :question/problem "The mass, in metric tons, of a meteor is modeled by the function $m(t)=20\\cdot (0.4)^{t},$ where $t$ represents the time, in minutes, since the meteor entered the Earth's atmosphere. In metric tons, how much mass did the meteor lose between the second and third minutes after entering the Earth's atmosphere?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the total mass that the meteor lost between $t=2$ and $t=3$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the meteor's mass after $2$ minutes, and we subtract its mass after $3$ minutes. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3).\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=2$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.16 \\\\  & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=3$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(3) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{3} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.064 \\\\  & =1.28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass lost between the second and third minute is\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3)=3.2-1.28=1.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the meteor's mass decreased by $1.92$ metric tons between the second and third minutes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114145 :answer-field/correct 17592186114149 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" :question/id #uuid "8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-45713" :question/problem "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" "answer-62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" "answer-120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" "answer-fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" "answer-9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" :answer/id #uuid "8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\,875" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer/id #uuid "62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46\\,875" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" :answer/id #uuid "120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" :answer/id #uuid "fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\,675" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c" :answer/id #uuid "9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115866 :question/problem "The market value of a sports car, in dollars, can be modeled according to the rule $30\\,000\\cdot (0.85)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the car was manufactured. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.85)$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=30\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.85,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.85\\,⟹\\,r=0.15.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.15$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $15%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.85$ means that the value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114133 :answer-field/correct 17592186114136 :answer-field/choices ["answer-78fd8c92-25de-5ac5-bef6-5099c54e2d5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78fd8c92-25de-5ac5-bef6-5099c54e2d5c" :answer/id #uuid "78fd8c92-25de-5ac5-bef6-5099c54e2d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The initial price of the car is $\\$25\\, 500$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" "question-25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" "question-cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" :question/id #uuid "9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" :question/math-academy-id "q-256827" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[-2,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2})^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x)^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-8{x}_{n}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-8{x}_{0}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})-8} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-8(2)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)-8} \\\\  & =0.505\\,007…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-8{x}_{1}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})-8} \\\\  & =(0.505\\,007…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)+(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8(0.505\\,007…)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8} \\\\  & ≈0.025,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-91adf0bc-26e8-5ef8-a01d-3f8b945f9b47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-91adf0bc-26e8-5ef8-a01d-3f8b945f9b47" :answer-field/id #uuid "91adf0bc-26e8-5ef8-a01d-3f8b945f9b47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer/id #uuid "452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.025" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" :question/id #uuid "25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" :question/math-academy-id "q-256858" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[0.5,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})^{′}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2{x}_{n})}{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2)}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1} \\\\  & =0.878\\,555…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})+1} \\\\  & =(0.878\\,555…)-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+0.878\\,555…}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+1} \\\\  & =0.901\\,873…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈0.879$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.902,$ rounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b875e44-dea9-5add-bcd9-2d829e35888d" "answer-field-0d10b84c-ddb7-53d8-af30-74088c715ba6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b875e44-dea9-5add-bcd9-2d829e35888d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b875e44-dea9-5add-bcd9-2d829e35888d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer/id #uuid "8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.879" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d10b84c-ddb7-53d8-af30-74088c715ba6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d10b84c-ddb7-53d8-af30-74088c715ba6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer/id #uuid "532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.902" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" :question/id #uuid "cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" :question/math-academy-id "q-256862" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{3}$ in the interval $[3,4].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=3.6$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-{x}^{3})^{′}={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =3.6-\\frac{{e}^{3.6}-3(3.6)^{2}}{{e}^{3.6}-6(3.6)} \\\\  & =3.752\\,135…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(3.752\\,135…)-\\frac{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-3(3.752\\,135…)^{2}}{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-6(3.752\\,135…)} \\\\  & =3.733\\,407…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈3.752$ and ${x}_{2}≈3.733,$ rounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eaa0b5de-3e08-5716-8224-20250363178e" "answer-field-8b467806-b4ba-5d03-bdb3-631ac4ebb45e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eaa0b5de-3e08-5716-8224-20250363178e" :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa0b5de-3e08-5716-8224-20250363178e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer/id #uuid "2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.752" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b467806-b4ba-5d03-bdb3-631ac4ebb45e" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b467806-b4ba-5d03-bdb3-631ac4ebb45e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer/id #uuid "a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.733" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" "question-4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" :question/id #uuid "2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-394147" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-12x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-12x)^{′}=6{x}^{5}-12, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-12)^{′}=30{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4})-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-12)}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{24{x}_{0}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.5)^{5}+12}{30(1.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.279\\,012…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{24{x}_{1}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.279\\,012…)^{5}+12}{30(1.279\\,012…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.172\\,682…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{24{x}_{2}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.172\\,682…)^{5}+12}{30(1.172\\,682…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.149\\,659…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{24{x}_{3}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.149\\,659…)^{5}+12}{30(1.149\\,659…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148\\,699….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.15$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98ec7d6b-6a31-5769-9cf5-7523c10eb966"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98ec7d6b-6a31-5769-9cf5-7523c10eb966" :answer-field/id #uuid "98ec7d6b-6a31-5769-9cf5-7523c10eb966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer-field/choices ["answer-57464dc7-bc2e-5b9c-aecb-331f510dce12" "answer-c08ae5fa-bc80-5a70-8390-cd92ea33a585" "answer-6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" "answer-6ac44f6e-0104-5745-83a5-1440a199990a" "answer-a83a0cc8-6739-57e1-83af-4f3206bf0afd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57464dc7-bc2e-5b9c-aecb-331f510dce12" :answer/id #uuid "57464dc7-bc2e-5b9c-aecb-331f510dce12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c08ae5fa-bc80-5a70-8390-cd92ea33a585" :answer/id #uuid "c08ae5fa-bc80-5a70-8390-cd92ea33a585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer/id #uuid "6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ac44f6e-0104-5745-83a5-1440a199990a" :answer/id #uuid "6ac44f6e-0104-5745-83a5-1440a199990a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a83a0cc8-6739-57e1-83af-4f3206bf0afd" :answer/id #uuid "a83a0cc8-6739-57e1-83af-4f3206bf0afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" :question/id #uuid "4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-394148" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5$ has a critical point in the interval $[2,4],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=3.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5)^{′}={e}^{x}-6x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6)-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}({x}_{0}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.5}(3.5-1)}{{e}^{3.5}-6} \\\\  & =3.053\\,190…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}(3.053\\,190…-1)}{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}-6} \\\\  & =2.864\\,577…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}(2.864\\,577…-1)}{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,890…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{3}}({x}_{3}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{3}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}(2.833\\,890…-1)}{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,149….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈2.83$ and ${x}_{4}≈2.83$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈2.83,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed233c25-8953-5cf6-8918-dc5828016abb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed233c25-8953-5cf6-8918-dc5828016abb" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed233c25-8953-5cf6-8918-dc5828016abb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c162ef2f-cc9b-56e9-8583-03470ec95cfa" "answer-190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" "answer-ba2ea8de-39f8-5969-9d65-3f5ca48f745d" "answer-00608277-e348-507e-8515-26d9d8435c11" "answer-95bbc949-7307-5b19-94e5-df2c13bf0a69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c162ef2f-cc9b-56e9-8583-03470ec95cfa" :answer/id #uuid "c162ef2f-cc9b-56e9-8583-03470ec95cfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈2.72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer/id #uuid "190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈2.83" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba2ea8de-39f8-5969-9d65-3f5ca48f745d" :answer/id #uuid "ba2ea8de-39f8-5969-9d65-3f5ca48f745d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈2.86" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00608277-e348-507e-8515-26d9d8435c11" :answer/id #uuid "00608277-e348-507e-8515-26d9d8435c11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈2.91" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95bbc949-7307-5b19-94e5-df2c13bf0a69" :answer/id #uuid "95bbc949-7307-5b19-94e5-df2c13bf0a69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈3.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d6344c23-c208-5a49-a584-a569b2952ea7" :activity/id #uuid "d6344c23-c208-5a49-a584-a569b2952ea7" :activity/title "Newton's Method for Optimizing Single-Variable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048628 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-f5ab5c9e-f782-5299-aa3a-8aea8958e708" "step-12de3978-4355-514f-ab7e-268d3e2d89e6" "step-e658d0bc-cfdb-54df-a117-9454d8f9d284" "step-22962013-5da4-5a8a-ae59-f0ec55f33f51" "step-9ac933f2-f01f-51d6-973c-c8ab7550c9d7"] :activity/first-step "step-f5ab5c9e-f782-5299-aa3a-8aea8958e708" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f5ab5c9e-f782-5299-aa3a-8aea8958e708" :step/id #uuid "f5ab5c9e-f782-5299-aa3a-8aea8958e708" :step/content "question-9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" :step/next "step-12de3978-4355-514f-ab7e-268d3e2d89e6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-12de3978-4355-514f-ab7e-268d3e2d89e6" :step/id #uuid "12de3978-4355-514f-ab7e-268d3e2d89e6" :step/content "question-25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" :step/next "step-e658d0bc-cfdb-54df-a117-9454d8f9d284" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e658d0bc-cfdb-54df-a117-9454d8f9d284" :step/id #uuid "e658d0bc-cfdb-54df-a117-9454d8f9d284" :step/content "question-cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" :step/next "step-22962013-5da4-5a8a-ae59-f0ec55f33f51" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-22962013-5da4-5a8a-ae59-f0ec55f33f51" :step/id #uuid "22962013-5da4-5a8a-ae59-f0ec55f33f51" :step/content "question-2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" :step/next "step-9ac933f2-f01f-51d6-973c-c8ab7550c9d7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9ac933f2-f01f-51d6-973c-c8ab7550c9d7" :step/id #uuid "9ac933f2-f01f-51d6-973c-c8ab7550c9d7" :step/content "question-4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" :question/id #uuid "cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" :question/math-academy-id "q-80911" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45a64cfe-8191-5b79-aaff-a5627b1f6695"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45a64cfe-8191-5b79-aaff-a5627b1f6695" :answer-field/id #uuid "45a64cfe-8191-5b79-aaff-a5627b1f6695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" "answer-fd03625b-a11f-58bd-a340-4e7487dfc2c8" "answer-da268bf5-cb3d-56a1-95c2-03e31ddbd921" "answer-f787f3d0-ca6e-5982-8896-c3df5a7de145" "answer-8e08605f-fa3a-569b-af1b-d45a70992915"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" :answer/id #uuid "d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd03625b-a11f-58bd-a340-4e7487dfc2c8" :answer/id #uuid "fd03625b-a11f-58bd-a340-4e7487dfc2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da268bf5-cb3d-56a1-95c2-03e31ddbd921" :answer/id #uuid "da268bf5-cb3d-56a1-95c2-03e31ddbd921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f787f3d0-ca6e-5982-8896-c3df5a7de145" :answer/id #uuid "f787f3d0-ca6e-5982-8896-c3df5a7de145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e08605f-fa3a-569b-af1b-d45a70992915" :answer/id #uuid "8e08605f-fa3a-569b-af1b-d45a70992915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-69e8d858-bc7b-50b8-b9ca-21c046adc840" :activity/id #uuid "69e8d858-bc7b-50b8-b9ca-21c046adc840" :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050450 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-8f062582-b92a-5ca2-a69b-5fcb27e36a6a" "step-292b00bf-bb40-5de2-a2ae-9b94332bbb33" "step-de1c04b6-3944-5a50-8bfe-e60e82727aab" "step-4371b504-a799-50b5-a82a-215dffd22710" "step-863071d9-8226-571f-9257-700dcf82f4a3"] :activity/first-step "step-8f062582-b92a-5ca2-a69b-5fcb27e36a6a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-8f062582-b92a-5ca2-a69b-5fcb27e36a6a" :step/id #uuid "8f062582-b92a-5ca2-a69b-5fcb27e36a6a" :step/content "question-cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" :step/next "step-292b00bf-bb40-5de2-a2ae-9b94332bbb33" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-292b00bf-bb40-5de2-a2ae-9b94332bbb33" :step/id #uuid "292b00bf-bb40-5de2-a2ae-9b94332bbb33" :step/content 17592186399138 :step/next "step-de1c04b6-3944-5a50-8bfe-e60e82727aab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de1c04b6-3944-5a50-8bfe-e60e82727aab" :step/id #uuid "de1c04b6-3944-5a50-8bfe-e60e82727aab" :step/content 17592186351188 :step/next "step-4371b504-a799-50b5-a82a-215dffd22710" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4371b504-a799-50b5-a82a-215dffd22710" :step/id #uuid "4371b504-a799-50b5-a82a-215dffd22710" :step/content 17592186398713 :step/next "step-863071d9-8226-571f-9257-700dcf82f4a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-863071d9-8226-571f-9257-700dcf82f4a3" :step/id #uuid "863071d9-8226-571f-9257-700dcf82f4a3" :step/content 17592186302267 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79cf6220-8640-5993-92c2-8195fa289578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167246 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x-3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-3xy)=2-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y & -3x \\\\ 4y & -5+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-3(0) & -3(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -5+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2eaeaa0f-93b6-5bc4-9ecd-6d615a4f70ad" "answer-field-c64201f3-9bf5-59d1-86fd-e3e37afa6d2c" "answer-field-f267bf00-331a-5ba4-80d1-f50c27b73d21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164547 :answer-field/correct 17592186164552 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164565 :answer-field/correct 17592186164570 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164559 :answer-field/correct 17592186164560 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164553 :answer-field/correct 17592186164558 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2eaeaa0f-93b6-5bc4-9ecd-6d615a4f70ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "2eaeaa0f-93b6-5bc4-9ecd-6d615a4f70ad" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7910179a-6843-55c8-9a4b-4d9acb40f753" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8108f31-ba67-5e50-82f4-6150ccaf853c" "answer-615e47fa-f053-5932-89ec-654b029d22cc" "answer-7910179a-6843-55c8-9a4b-4d9acb40f753" "answer-e697497c-675e-594f-9a80-f862cf2d3b11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8108f31-ba67-5e50-82f4-6150ccaf853c" :answer/id #uuid "e8108f31-ba67-5e50-82f4-6150ccaf853c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-615e47fa-f053-5932-89ec-654b029d22cc" :answer/id #uuid "615e47fa-f053-5932-89ec-654b029d22cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/va