d align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46896807-998c-5933-b1eb-99ca4e046406" "answer-field-9159810c-d2c6-59d8-84ab-dc3766a59aa8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46896807-998c-5933-b1eb-99ca4e046406" :answer-field/id #uuid "46896807-998c-5933-b1eb-99ca4e046406" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a272e7c-5ccc-58a1-bc0e-68ec31a26ddd" "answer-d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" "answer-e345ffd9-c7a8-5122-b489-3449540bacce" "answer-6c917360-af2a-547f-83e0-3ae046b64006"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a272e7c-5ccc-58a1-bc0e-68ec31a26ddd" :answer/id #uuid "9a272e7c-5ccc-58a1-bc0e-68ec31a26ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" :answer/id #uuid "d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e345ffd9-c7a8-5122-b489-3449540bacce" :answer/id #uuid "e345ffd9-c7a8-5122-b489-3449540bacce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c917360-af2a-547f-83e0-3ae046b64006" :answer/id #uuid "6c917360-af2a-547f-83e0-3ae046b64006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9159810c-d2c6-59d8-84ab-dc3766a59aa8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9159810c-d2c6-59d8-84ab-dc3766a59aa8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b53c207-98c0-5e8d-8dea-4637e64bc338" "answer-4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" "answer-8bb6b832-e503-5169-a52a-89ac629263af" "answer-f6eb6cc2-da68-5a29-9a26-d0470fcf0791"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b53c207-98c0-5e8d-8dea-4637e64bc338" :answer/id #uuid "1b53c207-98c0-5e8d-8dea-4637e64bc338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t+s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" :answer/id #uuid "4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bb6b832-e503-5169-a52a-89ac629263af" :answer/id #uuid "8bb6b832-e503-5169-a52a-89ac629263af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t+s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6eb6cc2-da68-5a29-9a26-d0470fcf0791" :answer/id #uuid "f6eb6cc2-da68-5a29-9a26-d0470fcf0791" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-88c3f0f4-9c38-5381-bb74-2b68573561ad" :activity/id #uuid "88c3f0f4-9c38-5381-bb74-2b68573561ad" :activity/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050707 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-dcfacae2-b140-5deb-bae8-f0b8d93c1f96" "step-098818fd-a8d8-5d01-87be-46e181cc7f9d" "step-0df3f949-5bbe-5136-b7e7-4edc6e213b6f" "step-de6d93f6-e851-58c8-a9b2-117e1ba0ae41"] :activity/first-step "step-dcfacae2-b140-5deb-bae8-f0b8d93c1f96" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-dcfacae2-b140-5deb-bae8-f0b8d93c1f96" :step/id #uuid "dcfacae2-b140-5deb-bae8-f0b8d93c1f96" :step/content "question-9f8ace62-6215-5f44-93f8-e30dfc0372e6" :step/next "step-098818fd-a8d8-5d01-87be-46e181cc7f9d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-098818fd-a8d8-5d01-87be-46e181cc7f9d" :step/id #uuid "098818fd-a8d8-5d01-87be-46e181cc7f9d" :step/content "question-f491a1e5-e94d-5b1b-9331-94aa4176665c" :step/next "step-0df3f949-5bbe-5136-b7e7-4edc6e213b6f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0df3f949-5bbe-5136-b7e7-4edc6e213b6f" :step/id #uuid "0df3f949-5bbe-5136-b7e7-4edc6e213b6f" :step/content 17592186229675 :step/next "step-de6d93f6-e851-58c8-a9b2-117e1ba0ae41" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de6d93f6-e851-58c8-a9b2-117e1ba0ae41" :step/id #uuid "de6d93f6-e851-58c8-a9b2-117e1ba0ae41" :step/content "question-8e6e37b0-420c-5604-b7d4-9e71462bd5eb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079080 :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{4}$ from ${a}_{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186077258 :answer-field/correct 17592186077263 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078424 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" "question-028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" :question/id #uuid "56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51839" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{9}=9$ and ${a}_{22}=178.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{9}=9,\\,{a}_{22}=178.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{9}}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{178-9}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{13} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=13.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-887edb48-4438-5fb8-9f68-ad1ecb484ac1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-887edb48-4438-5fb8-9f68-ad1ecb484ac1" :answer-field/id #uuid "887edb48-4438-5fb8-9f68-ad1ecb484ac1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-670cec8e-0d34-56b0-87fa-070c4615dfb0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-670cec8e-0d34-56b0-87fa-070c4615dfb0" "answer-fa518048-206a-54de-a58b-6bc20ddc9f4d" "answer-533597a1-e3f5-5fa1-94dc-91bba990baca" "answer-495383fd-96af-5901-b036-2fc2d4ed846d" "answer-54e4cfc3-636f-5122-be78-c9628acb2eb2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-670cec8e-0d34-56b0-87fa-070c4615dfb0" :answer/id #uuid "670cec8e-0d34-56b0-87fa-070c4615dfb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa518048-206a-54de-a58b-6bc20ddc9f4d" :answer/id #uuid "fa518048-206a-54de-a58b-6bc20ddc9f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533597a1-e3f5-5fa1-94dc-91bba990baca" :answer/id #uuid "533597a1-e3f5-5fa1-94dc-91bba990baca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-495383fd-96af-5901-b036-2fc2d4ed846d" :answer/id #uuid "495383fd-96af-5901-b036-2fc2d4ed846d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54e4cfc3-636f-5122-be78-c9628acb2eb2" :answer/id #uuid "54e4cfc3-636f-5122-be78-c9628acb2eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce" :question/id #uuid "028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-51867" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its seventh and twenty-second terms are $126$ and $-9$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{7}=126,\\,{a}_{22}=-9.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{7}}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)-126}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{-135}{15} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-618eab75-64d8-512a-bb20-45001701ac97"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-618eab75-64d8-512a-bb20-45001701ac97" :answer-field/id #uuid "618eab75-64d8-512a-bb20-45001701ac97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-657ee12f-f6f4-59d4-bac7-11a544c78598" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2564f09-d536-5dd1-b262-ee4b42fae3e0" "answer-3dafe0d7-7167-5df4-9ba9-18802932947e" "answer-657ee12f-f6f4-59d4-bac7-11a544c78598" "answer-6116e349-e0a5-58a8-8407-21b7d71d2915" "answer-d312637a-fb32-535d-b6c7-1fb61f66a8e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2564f09-d536-5dd1-b262-ee4b42fae3e0" :answer/id #uuid "d2564f09-d536-5dd1-b262-ee4b42fae3e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dafe0d7-7167-5df4-9ba9-18802932947e" :answer/id #uuid "3dafe0d7-7167-5df4-9ba9-18802932947e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-657ee12f-f6f4-59d4-bac7-11a544c78598" :answer/id #uuid "657ee12f-f6f4-59d4-bac7-11a544c78598" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6116e349-e0a5-58a8-8407-21b7d71d2915" :answer/id #uuid "6116e349-e0a5-58a8-8407-21b7d71d2915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d312637a-fb32-535d-b6c7-1fb61f66a8e0" :answer/id #uuid "d312637a-fb32-535d-b6c7-1fb61f66a8e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0b2ee7db-7c41-5b6b-a695-5915c75ef129" :activity/id #uuid "0b2ee7db-7c41-5b6b-a695-5915c75ef129" :activity/title "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048338 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-bb173ec4-dd61-50f2-8584-c82411d790aa" "step-8aad04a0-c6e7-572a-a7a1-bb3e0b3a6360" "step-dae43b25-3e8a-58c9-81da-f3af859a0e75"] :activity/first-step "step-bb173ec4-dd61-50f2-8584-c82411d790aa" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bb173ec4-dd61-50f2-8584-c82411d790aa" :step/id #uuid "bb173ec4-dd61-50f2-8584-c82411d790aa" :step/content 17592186079080 :step/next "step-8aad04a0-c6e7-572a-a7a1-bb3e0b3a6360" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8aad04a0-c6e7-572a-a7a1-bb3e0b3a6360" :step/id #uuid "8aad04a0-c6e7-572a-a7a1-bb3e0b3a6360" :step/content "question-56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" :step/next "step-dae43b25-3e8a-58c9-81da-f3af859a0e75" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dae43b25-3e8a-58c9-81da-f3af859a0e75" :step/id #uuid "dae43b25-3e8a-58c9-81da-f3af859a0e75" :step/content "question-028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167132 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167133 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}+3xy]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}18{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[6{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 48 & =(2a+3\\cdot 1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 8 & =2a+3 \\\\ 5 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{5}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163876 :answer-field/correct 17592186163880 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" :question/id #uuid "f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163156" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}6xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[12{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =(6a\\cdot 2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6dce9295-adc4-54f0-85bb-a7daa031279d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6dce9295-adc4-54f0-85bb-a7daa031279d" :answer-field/id #uuid "6dce9295-adc4-54f0-85bb-a7daa031279d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b26cb4c0-36e4-5059-b498-2f505edf4153" "answer-e442d8f5-617c-59ab-a4ac-822779b6d576" "answer-b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" "answer-33a00aa3-d6d0-54f6-84c3-a84dbc1e7955" "answer-9affac74-7d2b-5cbc-a27c-c731e04fc635"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b26cb4c0-36e4-5059-b498-2f505edf4153" :answer/id #uuid "b26cb4c0-36e4-5059-b498-2f505edf4153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e442d8f5-617c-59ab-a4ac-822779b6d576" :answer/id #uuid "e442d8f5-617c-59ab-a4ac-822779b6d576" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" :answer/id #uuid "b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33a00aa3-d6d0-54f6-84c3-a84dbc1e7955" :answer/id #uuid "33a00aa3-d6d0-54f6-84c3-a84dbc1e7955" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9affac74-7d2b-5cbc-a27c-c731e04fc635" :answer/id #uuid "9affac74-7d2b-5cbc-a27c-c731e04fc635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281634 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+2y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281635 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-99747c81-e556-573e-9a49-314528507d7a" :activity/id #uuid "99747c81-e556-573e-9a49-314528507d7a" :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049957 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" "step-2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" "step-837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" "step-99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" "step-c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab"] :activity/first-step "step-76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" :step/id #uuid "76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" :step/content 17592186167132 :step/next "step-2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" :step/id #uuid "2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" :step/content 17592186167133 :step/next "step-837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" :step/id #uuid "837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" :step/content "question-f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" :step/next "step-99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" :step/id #uuid "99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" :step/content 17592186281634 :step/next "step-c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab" :step/id #uuid "c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab" :step/content 17592186281635 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119756 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7769fe1f-a9ea-5777-8254-93dfa6c193aa" "question-9abf643c-c154-51d5-82bc-efb0d0062ad6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7769fe1f-a9ea-5777-8254-93dfa6c193aa" :question/id #uuid "7769fe1f-a9ea-5777-8254-93dfa6c193aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-53580" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f5f82876a00c72d7ec829f320d021613d1932b85ef87c7981f9f92669792cb9.png)\n\nJessica's circular swimming pool has a radius of $10\\,\\text{ft}.$ What is the area of the pool?" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ so the area of Jessica's pool is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a463388e-81ee-55c1-8529-9813db3a26dc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a463388e-81ee-55c1-8529-9813db3a26dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "a463388e-81ee-55c1-8529-9813db3a26dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0ecb785-4190-5d1d-bbfb-ddfba19efeab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0ecb785-4190-5d1d-bbfb-ddfba19efeab" "answer-76c02040-9874-5be6-8aa7-4bcf208d57ad" "answer-48dda0cc-a22f-5a91-861b-11ee881de9d8" "answer-756f53a0-e350-5fef-8684-70525d758686" "answer-3921910a-1de5-598a-93d7-2fdc2b7192d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0ecb785-4190-5d1d-bbfb-ddfba19efeab" :answer/id #uuid "e0ecb785-4190-5d1d-bbfb-ddfba19efeab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76c02040-9874-5be6-8aa7-4bcf208d57ad" :answer/id #uuid "76c02040-9874-5be6-8aa7-4bcf208d57ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48dda0cc-a22f-5a91-861b-11ee881de9d8" :answer/id #uuid "48dda0cc-a22f-5a91-861b-11ee881de9d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-756f53a0-e350-5fef-8684-70525d758686" :answer/id #uuid "756f53a0-e350-5fef-8684-70525d758686" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "400\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3921910a-1de5-598a-93d7-2fdc2b7192d7" :answer/id #uuid "3921910a-1de5-598a-93d7-2fdc2b7192d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9abf643c-c154-51d5-82bc-efb0d0062ad6" :question/id #uuid "9abf643c-c154-51d5-82bc-efb0d0062ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79020" :question/problem "![](images/22/22f63e5e602b29317d7df70f7dae7387feccdd9ab5678592e6c1af35d3d45fd3.png)\n\nWhat is the area of a circle that has a diameter of $8\\,\\text{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of a circle is half the diameter. So, the radius of our circle is $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15e0cf27-8090-59a9-82f4-1f188dede25c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15e0cf27-8090-59a9-82f4-1f188dede25c" :answer-field/id #uuid "15e0cf27-8090-59a9-82f4-1f188dede25c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b37fcd91-0cf3-59ac-ad10-6c9d508f701d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b37fcd91-0cf3-59ac-ad10-6c9d508f701d" "answer-4724aa93-864b-5977-9bce-176ade3fd8e4" "answer-c950e967-2717-54e9-80e2-d197f1792abe" "answer-3e038d9a-c7c9-554c-bf61-0c349d8c5da3" "answer-787de4de-39bc-5a72-95d5-54d7ad83eb88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b37fcd91-0cf3-59ac-ad10-6c9d508f701d" :answer/id #uuid "b37fcd91-0cf3-59ac-ad10-6c9d508f701d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4724aa93-864b-5977-9bce-176ade3fd8e4" :answer/id #uuid "4724aa93-864b-5977-9bce-176ade3fd8e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16{\\pi }^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c950e967-2717-54e9-80e2-d197f1792abe" :answer/id #uuid "c950e967-2717-54e9-80e2-d197f1792abe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e038d9a-c7c9-554c-bf61-0c349d8c5da3" :answer/id #uuid "3e038d9a-c7c9-554c-bf61-0c349d8c5da3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-787de4de-39bc-5a72-95d5-54d7ad83eb88" :answer/id #uuid "787de4de-39bc-5a72-95d5-54d7ad83eb88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64{\\pi }^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119861 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e818e98a-49f0-5632-a39a-42d0b7b18e71"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e818e98a-49f0-5632-a39a-42d0b7b18e71" :question/id #uuid "e818e98a-49f0-5632-a39a-42d0b7b18e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-79026" :question/problem "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $9\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 9\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 9\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot 3=6\\,\\text{km}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d3f482e-4b2a-591f-baf2-85798d1eb2b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d3f482e-4b2a-591f-baf2-85798d1eb2b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d3f482e-4b2a-591f-baf2-85798d1eb2b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1366b50a-55de-5aba-a99f-0003e4d3aa16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15fdb7ca-98a2-5ffb-9674-d7dc9e7774f7" "answer-0f0c126a-ad91-57d7-9ff4-9aaefd0fb031" "answer-1366b50a-55de-5aba-a99f-0003e4d3aa16" "answer-5d2424d9-e201-5bcd-919e-7363b78944cf" "answer-981deb0c-4d5e-5a68-9ff8-0f8cb94c55de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15fdb7ca-98a2-5ffb-9674-d7dc9e7774f7" :answer/id #uuid "15fdb7ca-98a2-5ffb-9674-d7dc9e7774f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{km}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f0c126a-ad91-57d7-9ff4-9aaefd0fb031" :answer/id #uuid "0f0c126a-ad91-57d7-9ff4-9aaefd0fb031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\text{km}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1366b50a-55de-5aba-a99f-0003e4d3aa16" :answer/id #uuid "1366b50a-55de-5aba-a99f-0003e4d3aa16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{km}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d2424d9-e201-5bcd-919e-7363b78944cf" :answer/id #uuid "5d2424d9-e201-5bcd-919e-7363b78944cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{km}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-981deb0c-4d5e-5a68-9ff8-0f8cb94c55de" :answer/id #uuid "981deb0c-4d5e-5a68-9ff8-0f8cb94c55de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,\\text{km}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5883c3cc-ac27-5f50-884f-9afbbf0f7b85" :activity/id #uuid "5883c3cc-ac27-5f50-884f-9afbbf0f7b85" :activity/title "Areas of Circles" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049225 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-2117f793-0d8c-55c2-96cb-7d576270d83e" "step-ba1713ee-8952-5ce9-b609-a0242977cbb3" "step-6f4bb269-14f4-5218-acd7-bb7816a8185c"] :activity/first-step "step-2117f793-0d8c-55c2-96cb-7d576270d83e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2117f793-0d8c-55c2-96cb-7d576270d83e" :step/id #uuid "2117f793-0d8c-55c2-96cb-7d576270d83e" :step/content "question-7769fe1f-a9ea-5777-8254-93dfa6c193aa" :step/next "step-ba1713ee-8952-5ce9-b609-a0242977cbb3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ba1713ee-8952-5ce9-b609-a0242977cbb3" :step/id #uuid "ba1713ee-8952-5ce9-b609-a0242977cbb3" :step/content "question-9abf643c-c154-51d5-82bc-efb0d0062ad6" :step/next "step-6f4bb269-14f4-5218-acd7-bb7816a8185c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6f4bb269-14f4-5218-acd7-bb7816a8185c" :step/id #uuid "6f4bb269-14f4-5218-acd7-bb7816a8185c" :step/content "question-e818e98a-49f0-5632-a39a-42d0b7b18e71" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" "question-c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" :question/id #uuid "1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" :question/math-academy-id "q-71958" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9164d315-5789-5468-b831-e821976b6b32"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9164d315-5789-5468-b831-e821976b6b32" :answer-field/id #uuid "9164d315-5789-5468-b831-e821976b6b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" "answer-97c20d2c-d45f-5b09-9f39-ebe08248b75a" "answer-13af737a-117c-5f32-93ba-e6c9200ca3d2" "answer-0576b7ba-7b60-5cde-90eb-b7b6e9fa9cd7" "answer-1fb99734-0a8c-580d-a0f8-4954f321ea9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer/id #uuid "a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97c20d2c-d45f-5b09-9f39-ebe08248b75a" :answer/id #uuid "97c20d2c-d45f-5b09-9f39-ebe08248b75a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13af737a-117c-5f32-93ba-e6c9200ca3d2" :answer/id #uuid "13af737a-117c-5f32-93ba-e6c9200ca3d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12(x-11)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0576b7ba-7b60-5cde-90eb-b7b6e9fa9cd7" :answer/id #uuid "0576b7ba-7b60-5cde-90eb-b7b6e9fa9cd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(2x-11)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fb99734-0a8c-580d-a0f8-4954f321ea9a" :answer/id #uuid "1fb99734-0a8c-580d-a0f8-4954f321ea9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6(2x-11)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" :question/id #uuid "67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107288" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(4)+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-add9725c-6d77-5248-a0ae-02fe439fa683"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-add9725c-6d77-5248-a0ae-02fe439fa683" :answer-field/id #uuid "add9725c-6d77-5248-a0ae-02fe439fa683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b3dc5f8-2374-5afe-8f07-96ed5f7a5da8" "answer-9404be8d-cb6c-53b2-8a36-eb362e775360" "answer-49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" "answer-95cb350d-4388-53ea-9992-f43b19639582" "answer-604e0e8a-a825-50c3-a298-9ae13e709a27"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b3dc5f8-2374-5afe-8f07-96ed5f7a5da8" :answer/id #uuid "9b3dc5f8-2374-5afe-8f07-96ed5f7a5da8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9404be8d-cb6c-53b2-8a36-eb362e775360" :answer/id #uuid "9404be8d-cb6c-53b2-8a36-eb362e775360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer/id #uuid "49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95cb350d-4388-53ea-9992-f43b19639582" :answer/id #uuid "95cb350d-4388-53ea-9992-f43b19639582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-604e0e8a-a825-50c3-a298-9ae13e709a27" :answer/id #uuid "604e0e8a-a825-50c3-a298-9ae13e709a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" :question/id #uuid "c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314205" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(6x-5)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=6x-5$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(6x-5)^{′}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =36(6x-5)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-258869c8-85b7-5a80-8cff-298b4785a0ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-258869c8-85b7-5a80-8cff-298b4785a0ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "258869c8-85b7-5a80-8cff-298b4785a0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer/id #uuid "5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36(6x-5)^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-eefeaacd-681e-5870-8db3-088073475fd0" :activity/id #uuid "eefeaacd-681e-5870-8db3-088073475fd0" :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051084 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-6ee4acf2-5857-55f8-bb3a-ded7762c8061" "step-fc153cbc-8b84-5906-bc61-6b445d0eb4ba" "step-cca24e3e-cdea-5e7c-b3c8-5f84dfb83ea6"] :activity/first-step "step-6ee4acf2-5857-55f8-bb3a-ded7762c8061" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6ee4acf2-5857-55f8-bb3a-ded7762c8061" :step/id #uuid "6ee4acf2-5857-55f8-bb3a-ded7762c8061" :step/content "question-1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" :step/next "step-fc153cbc-8b84-5906-bc61-6b445d0eb4ba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fc153cbc-8b84-5906-bc61-6b445d0eb4ba" :step/id #uuid "fc153cbc-8b84-5906-bc61-6b445d0eb4ba" :step/content "question-67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" :step/next "step-cca24e3e-cdea-5e7c-b3c8-5f84dfb83ea6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cca24e3e-cdea-5e7c-b3c8-5f84dfb83ea6" :step/id #uuid "cca24e3e-cdea-5e7c-b3c8-5f84dfb83ea6" :step/content "question-c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6466be13-c99b-575a-b71f-807fcb74c35a" "question-91f32261-8a78-5d95-90b2-1f1ea2788664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6466be13-c99b-575a-b71f-807fcb74c35a" :question/id #uuid "6466be13-c99b-575a-b71f-807fcb74c35a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140899" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4163137c-19d5-5760-830b-46baabfb4cde"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4163137c-19d5-5760-830b-46baabfb4cde" :answer-field/id #uuid "4163137c-19d5-5760-830b-46baabfb4cde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-698818d2-e349-5492-afe3-aea60ef5bcee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3accf69-6d02-5352-8e83-6b95feb715b3" "answer-815ef885-3b73-56cb-a23f-25aebe01a29b" "answer-c2a45735-8388-5d82-b9ed-399d067d6fe0" "answer-698818d2-e349-5492-afe3-aea60ef5bcee" "answer-6457f67b-2cba-511b-873a-5f3ef78d29ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3accf69-6d02-5352-8e83-6b95feb715b3" :answer/id #uuid "c3accf69-6d02-5352-8e83-6b95feb715b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-815ef885-3b73-56cb-a23f-25aebe01a29b" :answer/id #uuid "815ef885-3b73-56cb-a23f-25aebe01a29b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2a45735-8388-5d82-b9ed-399d067d6fe0" :answer/id #uuid "c2a45735-8388-5d82-b9ed-399d067d6fe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-698818d2-e349-5492-afe3-aea60ef5bcee" :answer/id #uuid "698818d2-e349-5492-afe3-aea60ef5bcee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6457f67b-2cba-511b-873a-5f3ef78d29ab" :answer/id #uuid "6457f67b-2cba-511b-873a-5f3ef78d29ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-91f32261-8a78-5d95-90b2-1f1ea2788664" :question/id #uuid "91f32261-8a78-5d95-90b2-1f1ea2788664" :question/math-academy-id "q-95925" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-187182c6-9d34-5c69-9f31-7c57fcb17c18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-187182c6-9d34-5c69-9f31-7c57fcb17c18" :answer-field/id #uuid "187182c6-9d34-5c69-9f31-7c57fcb17c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-95451385-1d98-53d7-90b6-0a8625460d0d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db6c4c84-7369-578b-ac17-3384a46ebfd9" "answer-b6d3106c-11c4-573d-9587-f67771622cce" "answer-95451385-1d98-53d7-90b6-0a8625460d0d" "answer-ad78c5c4-00bd-55ca-affe-51c6b67c8cd8" "answer-f492b520-52e3-5ae1-a34b-4db0cfb3fa3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db6c4c84-7369-578b-ac17-3384a46ebfd9" :answer/id #uuid "db6c4c84-7369-578b-ac17-3384a46ebfd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6d3106c-11c4-573d-9587-f67771622cce" :answer/id #uuid "b6d3106c-11c4-573d-9587-f67771622cce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95451385-1d98-53d7-90b6-0a8625460d0d" :answer/id #uuid "95451385-1d98-53d7-90b6-0a8625460d0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad78c5c4-00bd-55ca-affe-51c6b67c8cd8" :answer/id #uuid "ad78c5c4-00bd-55ca-affe-51c6b67c8cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f492b520-52e3-5ae1-a34b-4db0cfb3fa3c" :answer/id #uuid "f492b520-52e3-5ae1-a34b-4db0cfb3fa3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70" :question/id #uuid "220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-95938" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-640612ec-cca7-54d8-80ce-3588c7e329a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-640612ec-cca7-54d8-80ce-3588c7e329a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "640612ec-cca7-54d8-80ce-3588c7e329a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9b5e34c-4678-5dca-b523-4a58e93795c8" "answer-65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" "answer-21a0e737-5697-57a4-a73d-edbc478b30a3" "answer-45a303ab-287c-5d20-b61f-e565124eda57" "answer-ca91840c-4fe1-5797-837e-cf0d04ba1b59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9b5e34c-4678-5dca-b523-4a58e93795c8" :answer/id #uuid "e9b5e34c-4678-5dca-b523-4a58e93795c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" :answer/id #uuid "65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21a0e737-5697-57a4-a73d-edbc478b30a3" :answer/id #uuid "21a0e737-5697-57a4-a73d-edbc478b30a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a303ab-287c-5d20-b61f-e565124eda57" :answer/id #uuid "45a303ab-287c-5d20-b61f-e565124eda57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca91840c-4fe1-5797-837e-cf0d04ba1b59" :answer/id #uuid "ca91840c-4fe1-5797-837e-cf0d04ba1b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135098 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ee/ee731d95c111101559ad135429198dd7e0b14313272f0933b1c706006da2fbd2.svg)\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/82/824c39c97bb115768856d672b39be8f65d8b4ecbc2ee5d6ccc9f4943594cbacd.svg)\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/5b/5ba9887a4d9b47963af171e9275319a2564eca5a4ef8b3ef44978e2ff4b111d9.svg)\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overset{{A}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{{R}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135099 :tutorial/content "Let's now go through the QR factorization algorithm to find the factorization of the matrix\n\n![](images/f0/f0d468f6bce43491b3f97d10185f4393ae8e64198e24bb7708ad66ec57c73df4.svg)\n\n**Step 1**. Orthogonalize the columns of $A$ to get the columns of $Q.$ Using the Gram-Schmidt process, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**. Compute $R=Q^TA.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the QR factorization of $A$ is\n\n![](images/7f/7f0face80ec3823f73e958fae5b78b12641204a8858bb2c8a7677be9827b919d.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d742eff4-edcb-531d-a1ae-99b88035cdf2" "question-4c02183d-b4b3-51bb-97f0-443673f54a1a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d742eff4-edcb-531d-a1ae-99b88035cdf2" :question/id #uuid "d742eff4-edcb-531d-a1ae-99b88035cdf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32585" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6d2c7d3-a8d8-5bda-af43-0a8e471ca20e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6d2c7d3-a8d8-5bda-af43-0a8e471ca20e" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d2c7d3-a8d8-5bda-af43-0a8e471ca20e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebc248cf-0e7d-5338-ad36-68399abd2b84" "answer-f53189c6-65a6-5c30-87b8-d21e34f090bf" "answer-4f45f559-94dd-563f-b6eb-86c40bcad18c" "answer-a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" "answer-6135e766-b82e-5f99-857b-dc1d26563ce1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebc248cf-0e7d-5338-ad36-68399abd2b84" :answer/id #uuid "ebc248cf-0e7d-5338-ad36-68399abd2b84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f53189c6-65a6-5c30-87b8-d21e34f090bf" :answer/id #uuid "f53189c6-65a6-5c30-87b8-d21e34f090bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f45f559-94dd-563f-b6eb-86c40bcad18c" :answer/id #uuid "4f45f559-94dd-563f-b6eb-86c40bcad18c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" :answer/id #uuid "a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6135e766-b82e-5f99-857b-dc1d26563ce1" :answer/id #uuid "6135e766-b82e-5f99-857b-dc1d26563ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c02183d-b4b3-51bb-97f0-443673f54a1a" :question/id #uuid "4c02183d-b4b3-51bb-97f0-443673f54a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32571" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44515a13-3042-5dad-a7e1-fc7dbbca7de6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44515a13-3042-5dad-a7e1-fc7dbbca7de6" :answer-field/id #uuid "44515a13-3042-5dad-a7e1-fc7dbbca7de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6294613e-6b1e-58a0-a797-a8bc314e0e5c" "answer-1a55d4c8-ba65-5de7-84a2-4ee94b361461" "answer-82e69121-ee24-55ee-be9b-a4fee20e1b36" "answer-92f08831-eeff-53f3-8e72-199165e34fe2" "answer-b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6294613e-6b1e-58a0-a797-a8bc314e0e5c" :answer/id #uuid "6294613e-6b1e-58a0-a797-a8bc314e0e5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a55d4c8-ba65-5de7-84a2-4ee94b361461" :answer/id #uuid "1a55d4c8-ba65-5de7-84a2-4ee94b361461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82e69121-ee24-55ee-be9b-a4fee20e1b36" :answer/id #uuid "82e69121-ee24-55ee-be9b-a4fee20e1b36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f08831-eeff-53f3-8e72-199165e34fe2" :answer/id #uuid "92f08831-eeff-53f3-8e72-199165e34fe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2" :answer/id #uuid "b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120193 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=5,{u}_{33}=-6,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-30.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117319 :answer-field/correct 17592186117321 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/id #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75098" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/id #uuid "76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer-field/choices ["answer-360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" "answer-840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" "answer-d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" "answer-2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" "answer-c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/id #uuid "840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/id #uuid "d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/id #uuid "2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/id #uuid "c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" :question/id #uuid "9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32723" :question/problem "Given that\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ M & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" "answer-420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" "answer-c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" "answer-65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" "answer-7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" :answer/id #uuid "1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" :answer/id #uuid "420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" :answer/id #uuid "c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/id #uuid "65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a" :answer/id #uuid "7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115112 :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b6886faa1ecd42e9539b9f6867ff0f629f6ae99366d8b9f397f82c5212ebffd.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115292 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3aad7acd-4801-580e-a95b-e6983a14c705" "question-f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115115 :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3+x^2-8x-12,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x).$ To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & -8 & -12 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 12 \\\\  & 1 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-3} & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x+2)^2.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9f011708eb389611f4c12df70ddfd318039b58b5cbfd7fcb3980a906b0ad75.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115293 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" "question-de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115116 :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(x+1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(3-0)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*. If we were to expand the parentheses, we'd get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(3-x)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/14/14c1e6701c5f52f37788d22a5e09ba0bc92897ea64fa64ce0f3717115d9604af.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115294 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" "question-fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115117 :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-x^3 + 2x^2 + 7x + 4,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x).$ To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & 7 & 4 \\\\  &  & -4 & -8 & -4 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\ \\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\ f(x) & =-(x-4)({x}^{2}+2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = - (x-4)(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=-1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-4)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-4)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29091d81f99dc2b99787194bb158cc9a2f850241c5b50ae9ad5ec3f94f11603f.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115695 :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(x+4)(x+2)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=-4$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(0+4)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(4) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/02/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113136 :answer-field/correct 17592186113138 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217" :question/id #uuid "6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217" :question/math-academy-id "q-112131" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-561d7a9f-05aa-5181-8cd5-fc83e0f35882"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-561d7a9f-05aa-5181-8cd5-fc83e0f35882" :answer-field/id #uuid "561d7a9f-05aa-5181-8cd5-fc83e0f35882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1a591d1-98a7-5248-9112-9e31ca903999" "answer-7469e963-5028-57a9-906d-251e526d81f5" "answer-eae50afa-0ea3-5f8a-a64a-50bd9c93e83a" "answer-d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" "answer-06413641-cb6c-59bd-b7c7-a2ec01cc1f02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1a591d1-98a7-5248-9112-9e31ca903999" :answer/id #uuid "c1a591d1-98a7-5248-9112-9e31ca903999" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/21/21d44b71cc74249d45910247627ed30f0f090364b209ba56470546af39619c29.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7469e963-5028-57a9-906d-251e526d81f5" :answer/id #uuid "7469e963-5028-57a9-906d-251e526d81f5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/48/48cb7502e7985edd92acf72c52ebc056c2dfd823118fa2bbf910a6e7903597a7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae50afa-0ea3-5f8a-a64a-50bd9c93e83a" :answer/id #uuid "eae50afa-0ea3-5f8a-a64a-50bd9c93e83a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5f/5f87eac1680cf001b5cfe649d9aa18b713b69eade8d626b108d968b2d220ca3f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" :answer/id #uuid "d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06413641-cb6c-59bd-b7c7-a2ec01cc1f02" :answer/id #uuid "06413641-cb6c-59bd-b7c7-a2ec01cc1f02" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/eb/ebb182491e9eebf8509d3354d31ec6cda4af9921cc3e1c527ac4fe8c1e7f9bd0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3aad7acd-4801-580e-a95b-e6983a14c705" :question/id #uuid "3aad7acd-4801-580e-a95b-e6983a14c705" :question/math-academy-id "q-112145" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 18 \\\\  &  & -2 & 12 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}-6x+9\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(x-3)(x-3)=(x+2)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-2$ and a double root at $x=3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+2)(0-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,18),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8f99a7f-d42d-56d7-a9f1-5ae9c59b98af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8f99a7f-d42d-56d7-a9f1-5ae9c59b98af" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f99a7f-d42d-56d7-a9f1-5ae9c59b98af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" "answer-cc094071-c587-5788-abd9-0c387eaf9306" "answer-f54318ee-4bea-54a0-b85c-ec775d748a48" "answer-e81ef1d7-aa58-5637-913c-7c96c2f820d4" "answer-00ae4acd-da40-5a3a-a650-919674ee0667"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" :answer/id #uuid "ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc094071-c587-5788-abd9-0c387eaf9306" :answer/id #uuid "cc094071-c587-5788-abd9-0c387eaf9306" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2e/2e5b06b196bba9019b3ea2f33e372ca05cb791bd09f42e32954efe97fda05135.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f54318ee-4bea-54a0-b85c-ec775d748a48" :answer/id #uuid "f54318ee-4bea-54a0-b85c-ec775d748a48" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/73/7384284052f745b189b7a127337cd133397e48dca2a2c8c7908c8ab36de46b19.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e81ef1d7-aa58-5637-913c-7c96c2f820d4" :answer/id #uuid "e81ef1d7-aa58-5637-913c-7c96c2f820d4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7d/7dd3a15f3dc313e1234e388432c974dbc2052878e67783f2afea5ac570a29f53.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00ae4acd-da40-5a3a-a650-919674ee0667" :answer/id #uuid "00ae4acd-da40-5a3a-a650-919674ee0667" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/9c/9c205eb2555ee72e52142aa0f97037b8ca1690d188e7fadb7e384c949817d037.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d" :question/id #uuid "f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112137" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 9 & -4 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-5x+4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-5x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x-4)=(x-4)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-4)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff339910-4fbb-5844-b100-4c1b4c7881f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff339910-4fbb-5844-b100-4c1b4c7881f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff339910-4fbb-5844-b100-4c1b4c7881f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f3a6199-7669-5754-afee-1653731f9e10" "answer-dd539f80-f263-5f13-b6da-89c7d1498a69" "answer-3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" "answer-11730dda-46b1-59fe-b211-2faca9c98f74" "answer-af81d9b5-26a0-5bd7-aa2b-1fb2d737fc1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f3a6199-7669-5754-afee-1653731f9e10" :answer/id #uuid "0f3a6199-7669-5754-afee-1653731f9e10" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0e/0e8cc20b39330100b9ce1b2e258f41527f8d7ebbaa4050f87838d68c98b41e53.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd539f80-f263-5f13-b6da-89c7d1498a69" :answer/id #uuid "dd539f80-f263-5f13-b6da-89c7d1498a69" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5a/5a569d7c9c245db3c5c06772b703956c1d021f8d7d1c08bea071cc071499b812.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" :answer/id #uuid "3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11730dda-46b1-59fe-b211-2faca9c98f74" :answer/id #uuid "11730dda-46b1-59fe-b211-2faca9c98f74" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/88/8835508bde7ea7167978fa3d17243daeef591fd36c4ed72538420127b3f7750d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af81d9b5-26a0-5bd7-aa2b-1fb2d737fc1e" :answer/id #uuid "af81d9b5-26a0-5bd7-aa2b-1fb2d737fc1e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bf/bf493c2853bf7d4e229f2b66430c5a676478ff5fb158b35685b32d266257f883.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" :question/id #uuid "ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" :question/math-academy-id "q-75155" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)(3+0)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c3ed2cb-8888-5412-974a-3f9e4de7b1f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c3ed2cb-8888-5412-974a-3f9e4de7b1f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3ed2cb-8888-5412-974a-3f9e4de7b1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0562bacd-93f9-54b9-b7b8-73da02fa84b1" "answer-a78a6516-11f2-5751-b18e-f936666d6657" "answer-c26a74fb-36d6-5500-8dd2-c54665870413" "answer-cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" "answer-d72140f3-dba4-5ef4-a028-fda2b3798dfc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0562bacd-93f9-54b9-b7b8-73da02fa84b1" :answer/id #uuid "0562bacd-93f9-54b9-b7b8-73da02fa84b1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3e/3ec4938c8981dce125d97c031240e3a4b4ee6cf2ca7e60c7ca7717b44bff7511.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a78a6516-11f2-5751-b18e-f936666d6657" :answer/id #uuid "a78a6516-11f2-5751-b18e-f936666d6657" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/44/440f59b2b5ec62f140b60f91e96d39d85bf4b708972ffaa7a1173da46065598e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c26a74fb-36d6-5500-8dd2-c54665870413" :answer/id #uuid "c26a74fb-36d6-5500-8dd2-c54665870413" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/62/626880abe7c3c6c89453d63701193715e7f5ad437e75ffb4d821caad072d96d8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" :answer/id #uuid "cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d72140f3-dba4-5ef4-a028-fda2b3798dfc" :answer/id #uuid "d72140f3-dba4-5ef4-a028-fda2b3798dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ec/ecd8c42e621eb97647249c42cc172c74f3adedbc4a6312ece1e0123013572ab3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c" :question/id #uuid "de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c" :question/math-academy-id "q-27994" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(2-x)(1-x)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(2-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1,$ and\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(2-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ee28e45-0132-5eed-a906-a92a5d72c6e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ee28e45-0132-5eed-a906-a92a5d72c6e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee28e45-0132-5eed-a906-a92a5d72c6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36e29128-9612-5621-b4dd-57ec7ce0df13" "answer-500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" "answer-f2c572a9-faaf-5aa7-9320-38b9b98cef3e" "answer-6fea984a-57b4-5fc2-8d51-df4673eb7fa0" "answer-3bcb05d7-50cf-50a3-bc08-d6e5f0e459e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36e29128-9612-5621-b4dd-57ec7ce0df13" :answer/id #uuid "36e29128-9612-5621-b4dd-57ec7ce0df13" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/37/37a1389b38a3961bfd810ae221f4ed20aa8ee6186f2dbf8ec6ce9543ab90c3d3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" :answer/id #uuid "500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2c572a9-faaf-5aa7-9320-38b9b98cef3e" :answer/id #uuid "f2c572a9-faaf-5aa7-9320-38b9b98cef3e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7e/7e1789d9797aab42d961e451ba33fd1e65f41e4b0fbebe86f51968a0460018a1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fea984a-57b4-5fc2-8d51-df4673eb7fa0" :answer/id #uuid "6fea984a-57b4-5fc2-8d51-df4673eb7fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a8/a87746ce937bdf3e9b4153b3114d360fa6ca0358ac5ac648d7c43a77f87f6c5d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bcb05d7-50cf-50a3-bc08-d6e5f0e459e5" :answer/id #uuid "3bcb05d7-50cf-50a3-bc08-d6e5f0e459e5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d2/d2d8ced0961eb8c526b2cc2b82518fe039c67fae63ca0b76d8aaaa9bca1e1b4a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" :question/id #uuid "8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75165" :question/problem "Given that $x-4$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-32x+32$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 10 & -32 & 32 \\\\  &  & -4 & 24 & -32 \\\\  & -1 & 6 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-6x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)({x}^{2}-6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)(x-2)(x-4)=-(x-2)(x-4)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-2)(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,32),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aae66bc5dcf29e5e85e33942541b510d136b4f7f13d633f28c46d4b5a9ca977b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7abee97d-691d-584c-b315-998bdc941618"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7abee97d-691d-584c-b315-998bdc941618" :answer-field/id #uuid "7abee97d-691d-584c-b315-998bdc941618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64e81793-590f-5b29-ac89-461f32a3e98f" "answer-2d476839-88f3-5d95-af60-1c6347ff4ab5" "answer-d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" "answer-fcea6cc2-ba7c-528e-b045-a8d2f1343f94" "answer-91d89bba-6aea-5df5-bb7c-dac9538b260b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64e81793-590f-5b29-ac89-461f32a3e98f" :answer/id #uuid "64e81793-590f-5b29-ac89-461f32a3e98f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/06/06b7c566fce9906b86314ed7e2e8655e63c16a67247041dd658b209f93e699a2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d476839-88f3-5d95-af60-1c6347ff4ab5" :answer/id #uuid "2d476839-88f3-5d95-af60-1c6347ff4ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1b/1bf83d0704c39f940bef9d32518fb89903ec5e68040a8d60df6c8a18194cde6b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" :answer/id #uuid "d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/88/88f17b45290320d63748005ee10d60441a2eee5f4936d286cb984aa890271bde.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcea6cc2-ba7c-528e-b045-a8d2f1343f94" :answer/id #uuid "fcea6cc2-ba7c-528e-b045-a8d2f1343f94" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/89/898496eef07ddef338c737819dba7d34db4fe58f8860c62902204ee1d70583f8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91d89bba-6aea-5df5-bb7c-dac9538b260b" :answer/id #uuid "91d89bba-6aea-5df5-bb7c-dac9538b260b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f4/f4441651a590fc45f3d42832fb9247d6932fe2999a3c4310d504c7bb53ee0e3a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a" :question/id #uuid "fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a" :question/math-academy-id "q-112166" :question/problem "Given that $x+2$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+8x+12,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 & 8 & 12 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & -12 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+2} & =-{x}^{2}+x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+2)(x+2)(x-3)=-(x-3)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da2e86d8-7763-51a4-ae33-886c3ae607fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da2e86d8-7763-51a4-ae33-886c3ae607fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "da2e86d8-7763-51a4-ae33-886c3ae607fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-235ba100-d4c1-5618-8234-09762289b2aa" "answer-121516ae-44e0-52e3-948a-fbb82bd8420e" "answer-c4c5a969-e226-575d-8194-95b240094b76" "answer-61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" "answer-3d9a3f71-fa92-5198-a731-22611ab2f298"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-235ba100-d4c1-5618-8234-09762289b2aa" :answer/id #uuid "235ba100-d4c1-5618-8234-09762289b2aa" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/16/16cb1fd207d56c24e409af4a559b6401627574772c27078d808a08459aa57f37.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-121516ae-44e0-52e3-948a-fbb82bd8420e" :answer/id #uuid "121516ae-44e0-52e3-948a-fbb82bd8420e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/48/48e4fc68dcd32d250158e27f873af285bc88fa30ff959f164c2db30d7c63e9e0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4c5a969-e226-575d-8194-95b240094b76" :answer/id #uuid "c4c5a969-e226-575d-8194-95b240094b76" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/51/51d68b8563124c14cdd03a299fcd402225fee11e91094db1d889157e13db9817.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" :answer/id #uuid "61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d9a3f71-fa92-5198-a731-22611ab2f298" :answer/id #uuid "3d9a3f71-fa92-5198-a731-22611ab2f298" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b1/b18e2b945ba8555b4a6de2d8fed5a394fa3bfb8af299c0a22c15fe4ecda2564d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116521 :tutorial/content "The graph of any cubic function $y = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d,$ where $a >0,$ has a characteristic inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $-\\infty$ to $+\\infty.$ This means that every such function has *at least* one real root and *at most* three distinct roots. \n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of $y = x^3-4x^2 +4x.$ First, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product rule, we get two distinct roots: $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The root $x=0$ is a *single* root: it has multiplicity ${\\color{blue}1}$ because it comes from a factor $x^{\\color{blue}1}$ whose exponent is ${\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- The root $x=2$ is a *double* root: it has multiplicity ${\\color{blue}2}$ because it comes from a factor $(x-2)^{\\color{blue}2}$ whose exponent is ${\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nTo graph the curve, we use the following principles:\n\n- The curve passes through the $x$-axis at the single root (in this case, $x=0$).\n\n- The curve touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root (in this case, $x=2$).\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9d/9d3c04ff9f84bef0499b30dfd51da7efd21afe48ad323e57ff8ae9cf6a999fe7.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116522 :tutorial/content "The graph of any cubic function $y = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d,$ where $a <0,$ has a characteristic ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $+\\infty$ to $-\\infty.$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of $y = -x^3+8x^2-16x.$ Then we set $y=0$ to find the roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}-16x & =0 \\\\ -x({x}^{2}-8x+16) & =0 \\\\ -x(x-4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product rule, we get two distinct roots: $x=0$ and $x=4,$ where $x=0$ is a single root and $x=4$ is a double root.\n\nTo graph the curve, we use the following principles:\n\n- The curve passes through the $x$-axis at the single root (in this case, $x=0$).\n\n- The curve touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root (in this case, $x=4$).\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/21/215fe4cdd8949bd91e826b3c1410c20d459557a4681fa856d02b9cfb6ab0b059.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115448 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162109 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-76dee0de-583e-58f7-b45a-1c6693fa7da9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-76dee0de-583e-58f7-b45a-1c6693fa7da9" :question/id #uuid "76dee0de-583e-58f7-b45a-1c6693fa7da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143273" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{49}∼N(-7,{14}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=49$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=49,\\,\\mu =-7,\\,\\sigma =14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-7,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{49}) \\\\  & ∼N(-7,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc48573d-1916-5467-95ee-4146ec74919f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc48573d-1916-5467-95ee-4146ec74919f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc48573d-1916-5467-95ee-4146ec74919f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78901c4c-e21d-5407-8221-99cca3e5f496" :answer-field/choices ["answer-808484b3-c4e2-5ada-9d42-a4f7e35e19c3" "answer-78901c4c-e21d-5407-8221-99cca3e5f496" "answer-69972084-40e8-582f-bcc5-0b0e3e4215cd" "answer-731438a1-6842-5b20-8b46-d913bcf42494" "answer-fa524b14-85fc-5527-8df6-5dc86467397e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-808484b3-c4e2-5ada-9d42-a4f7e35e19c3" :answer/id #uuid "808484b3-c4e2-5ada-9d42-a4f7e35e19c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-7,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78901c4c-e21d-5407-8221-99cca3e5f496" :answer/id #uuid "78901c4c-e21d-5407-8221-99cca3e5f496" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-7,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69972084-40e8-582f-bcc5-0b0e3e4215cd" :answer/id #uuid "69972084-40e8-582f-bcc5-0b0e3e4215cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-7,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731438a1-6842-5b20-8b46-d913bcf42494" :answer/id #uuid "731438a1-6842-5b20-8b46-d913bcf42494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(49,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa524b14-85fc-5527-8df6-5dc86467397e" :answer/id #uuid "fa524b14-85fc-5527-8df6-5dc86467397e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(49,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162110 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9af82014-8871-57e9-a191-d8c7d4765bcf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9af82014-8871-57e9-a191-d8c7d4765bcf" :question/id #uuid "9af82014-8871-57e9-a191-d8c7d4765bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-143282" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{24}∼N(-5,{12}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $24$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5160\\,$ | $\\,0.5199\\,$ | $\\,0.5239\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{{12}^{2}}{24}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6) & =P(Z<\\frac{-4.6-(-5)}{\\sqrt{6}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.16) \\\\  & =Φ(0.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.16)=0.5636.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.6) & =Φ(0.16) \\\\  & =0.5636.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62624d95-3bee-5a30-bc60-a0cc59009ddb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62624d95-3bee-5a30-bc60-a0cc59009ddb" :answer-field/id #uuid "62624d95-3bee-5a30-bc60-a0cc59009ddb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4278443-dd67-57ab-b8fa-01d209aa6fad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-034dd1c3-df1a-5890-87e5-be87cdbeaa91" "answer-f68cad25-edc1-55df-a1b3-90aa82dbf886" "answer-a6b1cc1c-ea01-5dff-8644-c0130fede926" "answer-d4278443-dd67-57ab-b8fa-01d209aa6fad" "answer-7f47c7a7-35eb-5c6c-bd05-bf7dca91bfef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-034dd1c3-df1a-5890-87e5-be87cdbeaa91" :answer/id #uuid "034dd1c3-df1a-5890-87e5-be87cdbeaa91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5160" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f68cad25-edc1-55df-a1b3-90aa82dbf886" :answer/id #uuid "f68cad25-edc1-55df-a1b3-90aa82dbf886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5199" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6b1cc1c-ea01-5dff-8644-c0130fede926" :answer/id #uuid "a6b1cc1c-ea01-5dff-8644-c0130fede926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5557" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4278443-dd67-57ab-b8fa-01d209aa6fad" :answer/id #uuid "d4278443-dd67-57ab-b8fa-01d209aa6fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5636" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f47c7a7-35eb-5c6c-bd05-bf7dca91bfef" :answer/id #uuid "7f47c7a7-35eb-5c6c-bd05-bf7dca91bfef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5987" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e71938ca-d172-5101-bd1a-d75c42be7cd7" :activity/id #uuid "e71938ca-d172-5101-bd1a-d75c42be7cd7" :activity/title "Sample Means From Normal Populations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049180 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-c367f721-dd49-5ab3-b6cd-7ae47ce18f57" "step-41bf49f4-f1a1-5bf2-bcf2-e8423f122466" "step-bd267e6d-06bf-5bdf-99e6-eec263cbb1a9" "step-dc663392-3491-5595-ae96-816cd6d6c65f" "step-cc547582-4274-5668-9d7a-413d0f8594d6"] :activity/first-step "step-c367f721-dd49-5ab3-b6cd-7ae47ce18f57" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c367f721-dd49-5ab3-b6cd-7ae47ce18f57" :step/id #uuid "c367f721-dd49-5ab3-b6cd-7ae47ce18f57" :step/content "question-76dee0de-583e-58f7-b45a-1c6693fa7da9" :step/next "step-41bf49f4-f1a1-5bf2-bcf2-e8423f122466" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-41bf49f4-f1a1-5bf2-bcf2-e8423f122466" :step/id #uuid "41bf49f4-f1a1-5bf2-bcf2-e8423f122466" :step/content 17592186498591 :step/next "step-bd267e6d-06bf-5bdf-99e6-eec263cbb1a9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bd267e6d-06bf-5bdf-99e6-eec263cbb1a9" :step/id #uuid "bd267e6d-06bf-5bdf-99e6-eec263cbb1a9" :step/content "question-9af82014-8871-57e9-a191-d8c7d4765bcf" :step/next "step-dc663392-3491-5595-ae96-816cd6d6c65f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dc663392-3491-5595-ae96-816cd6d6c65f" :step/id #uuid "dc663392-3491-5595-ae96-816cd6d6c65f" :step/content 17592186498578 :step/next "step-cc547582-4274-5668-9d7a-413d0f8594d6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cc547582-4274-5668-9d7a-413d0f8594d6" :step/id #uuid "cc547582-4274-5668-9d7a-413d0f8594d6" :step/content 17592186498499 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129108 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb" :question/id #uuid "c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37888" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef42744182deb0d83704b13fba2324b92df197151c3566de01bb75a202e836cc.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(\\frac{1}{3})=0.$\n\nTherefore, the only root of the function is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e84544a2-76c0-5219-9d3a-4068e68e816b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e84544a2-76c0-5219-9d3a-4068e68e816b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e84544a2-76c0-5219-9d3a-4068e68e816b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" "answer-956e4771-73f0-5b6f-980d-af1813caa099" "answer-1cd9eaf3-ed30-5541-af86-8a86b83910ca" "answer-6e2ccad8-734d-582e-b7c7-0ed5544f1b87" "answer-2e59e379-49a8-54d1-a572-f41f95712c03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" :answer/id #uuid "8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-956e4771-73f0-5b6f-980d-af1813caa099" :answer/id #uuid "956e4771-73f0-5b6f-980d-af1813caa099" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-4$ and $-\\frac{1}{9}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cd9eaf3-ed30-5541-af86-8a86b83910ca" :answer/id #uuid "1cd9eaf3-ed30-5541-af86-8a86b83910ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0$ and $\\frac{1}{7}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2ccad8-734d-582e-b7c7-0ed5544f1b87" :answer/id #uuid "6e2ccad8-734d-582e-b7c7-0ed5544f1b87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e59e379-49a8-54d1-a572-f41f95712c03" :answer/id #uuid "2e59e379-49a8-54d1-a572-f41f95712c03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no roots" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129107 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" "question-f7198a9b-72ee-54fc-b7ac-cda83b608de8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" :question/id #uuid "30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82668" :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ | $50$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $5$ | $15$ | $25$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $-25$ | $0$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $g(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $g(40)=0$ and $g(70)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=40$ and $x=70.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-112eb8e3-a60e-524c-8724-a81064b58bcb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-112eb8e3-a60e-524c-8724-a81064b58bcb" :answer-field/id #uuid "112eb8e3-a60e-524c-8724-a81064b58bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-989608c8-9218-5c75-88f5-8e4e4b9c8cf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9