bdc6f" :step/id #uuid "3cfceae3-a898-59b0-bb61-c360e1ebdc6f" :step/content "question-267a0513-367c-516d-9933-b0be78b78e58" :step/next "step-13841e88-b264-587e-a3ba-4dee503e95bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-13841e88-b264-587e-a3ba-4dee503e95bf" :step/id #uuid "13841e88-b264-587e-a3ba-4dee503e95bf" :step/content "question-0d0ef510-de0c-500a-8eb5-ba614cc6f88f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fa1dbe09-82df-56ed-9d56-cd22c9d94295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa1dbe09-82df-56ed-9d56-cd22c9d94295" :question/id #uuid "fa1dbe09-82df-56ed-9d56-cd22c9d94295" :question/math-academy-id "q-343762" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{2(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -4 & =A{e}^{2}+0 \\\\ A & =-4{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-4{e}^{-2}){e}^{2x}=-4{e}^{2x-2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-4{e}^{2x-2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11a67b6d-bb47-5970-9dcc-9c62063e0137"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11a67b6d-bb47-5970-9dcc-9c62063e0137" :answer-field/id #uuid "11a67b6d-bb47-5970-9dcc-9c62063e0137" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-48761810-bee9-52b5-b865-eda8411513f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48761810-bee9-52b5-b865-eda8411513f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48761810-bee9-52b5-b865-eda8411513f7" :answer/id #uuid "48761810-bee9-52b5-b865-eda8411513f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4e^{2x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c9b0aad8-60f7-5ec4-9cbf-7d3e0f654c83"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9b0aad8-60f7-5ec4-9cbf-7d3e0f654c83" :question/id #uuid "c9b0aad8-60f7-5ec4-9cbf-7d3e0f654c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-343986" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+6=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =B\\sqrt{6}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{6} & =5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a05e5f3c-b90d-5d46-940c-660c7f3ac6e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a05e5f3c-b90d-5d46-940c-660c7f3ac6e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a05e5f3c-b90d-5d46-940c-660c7f3ac6e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-16697760-02d1-543e-b475-0aedfdfe973c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6d2f260-c0b9-592f-80e3-d08f1f1c5004" "answer-5ded1cdb-2297-52b0-b33b-82891e1e11e2" "answer-12587c01-8aed-5ac1-86ba-15ed1938d516" "answer-16697760-02d1-543e-b475-0aedfdfe973c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6d2f260-c0b9-592f-80e3-d08f1f1c5004" :answer/id #uuid "a6d2f260-c0b9-592f-80e3-d08f1f1c5004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ded1cdb-2297-52b0-b33b-82891e1e11e2" :answer/id #uuid "5ded1cdb-2297-52b0-b33b-82891e1e11e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(5-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{b}_{n})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12587c01-8aed-5ac1-86ba-15ed1938d516" :answer/id #uuid "12587c01-8aed-5ac1-86ba-15ed1938d516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16697760-02d1-543e-b475-0aedfdfe973c" :answer/id #uuid "16697760-02d1-543e-b475-0aedfdfe973c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi {b}_{n}}{2\\sqrt{6}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c3ec228e-f685-546c-b58b-d16cfab842db" :activity/id #uuid "c3ec228e-f685-546c-b58b-d16cfab842db" :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049479 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-9ddccce4-192e-50de-93cc-26e35206d845" "step-c8d1ef2d-eade-557d-ba2e-fd425547f0b7" "step-0f3e5941-5ae6-550c-8cd9-e1b7424c0bfe" "step-edbe62c7-2be5-5fd2-a94a-d15566853dd7" "step-64ea9f3b-4819-57db-bf92-48e86b5d7d39"] :activity/first-step "step-9ddccce4-192e-50de-93cc-26e35206d845" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9ddccce4-192e-50de-93cc-26e35206d845" :step/id #uuid "9ddccce4-192e-50de-93cc-26e35206d845" :step/content 17592186231565 :step/next "step-c8d1ef2d-eade-557d-ba2e-fd425547f0b7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c8d1ef2d-eade-557d-ba2e-fd425547f0b7" :step/id #uuid "c8d1ef2d-eade-557d-ba2e-fd425547f0b7" :step/content 17592186365942 :step/next "step-0f3e5941-5ae6-550c-8cd9-e1b7424c0bfe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0f3e5941-5ae6-550c-8cd9-e1b7424c0bfe" :step/id #uuid "0f3e5941-5ae6-550c-8cd9-e1b7424c0bfe" :step/content "question-fa1dbe09-82df-56ed-9d56-cd22c9d94295" :step/next "step-edbe62c7-2be5-5fd2-a94a-d15566853dd7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-edbe62c7-2be5-5fd2-a94a-d15566853dd7" :step/id #uuid "edbe62c7-2be5-5fd2-a94a-d15566853dd7" :step/content "question-c9b0aad8-60f7-5ec4-9cbf-7d3e0f654c83" :step/next "step-64ea9f3b-4819-57db-bf92-48e86b5d7d39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-64ea9f3b-4819-57db-bf92-48e86b5d7d39" :step/id #uuid "64ea9f3b-4819-57db-bf92-48e86b5d7d39" :step/content 17592186365954 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-487e5162-7f53-555d-bd23-288509fce232"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba" :question/id #uuid "228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-18525" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9738ccf12d8b9e8f3ed84870371972767e67a624f31fe5782f39679253965c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,4)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50488e0d-3249-550c-8d98-bec9f2011aab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50488e0d-3249-550c-8d98-bec9f2011aab" :answer-field/id #uuid "50488e0d-3249-550c-8d98-bec9f2011aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29951222-87d7-548a-9a5e-144857424fb1" "answer-9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" "answer-e276d83b-7cf3-5d6b-86cd-b9f78e932f09" "answer-b92f6a22-272b-56d9-abd2-b059ce0496cb" "answer-46324b89-8e6d-58d2-acb1-a3d8251446db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29951222-87d7-548a-9a5e-144857424fb1" :answer/id #uuid "29951222-87d7-548a-9a5e-144857424fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer/id #uuid "9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e276d83b-7cf3-5d6b-86cd-b9f78e932f09" :answer/id #uuid "e276d83b-7cf3-5d6b-86cd-b9f78e932f09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b92f6a22-272b-56d9-abd2-b059ce0496cb" :answer/id #uuid "b92f6a22-272b-56d9-abd2-b059ce0496cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46324b89-8e6d-58d2-acb1-a3d8251446db" :answer/id #uuid "46324b89-8e6d-58d2-acb1-a3d8251446db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-487e5162-7f53-555d-bd23-288509fce232" :question/id #uuid "487e5162-7f53-555d-bd23-288509fce232" :question/math-academy-id "q-27526" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f5/f5d15cae5283eb02d555bee57f399dd00c58ec319413ca5817b1edba1ac69fb9.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8e997035826ffcb6969c5bbdda46e256234fb5720f45aa281719be631929d4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4b796fd-2386-5360-a29c-d6f97f457e1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4b796fd-2386-5360-a29c-d6f97f457e1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b796fd-2386-5360-a29c-d6f97f457e1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" "answer-cbaf05ab-28d2-5ec9-bcfe-eb752c46cfe4" "answer-1bb02bbf-e4db-51d2-820e-c820cd6c3132" "answer-2307cbd4-8b2c-507e-9d61-ab458dea3b70" "answer-d5cc661e-81a0-5227-89bf-6972cafbeab5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" :answer/id #uuid "19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/37/374572e9b9313502b6d806d37b095320496dc65d4e6383ea509b3ec30bc1a